Introduction
Problem statement and research motivation: 
Fetal health is monitored through Cardiotocography (CTG) charts, but interpreting these charts remains a challenge. Even experienced doctors misinterpate them, which potentially leads to incorrect diagnoses or unnecessary surgeries (Ayres-de-Campos et al., 2000). The recent advancement in AI models can predict the results through large number of variables but it remains unclear if fundamental markers work on their own. In this study, we investigate whether the baseline heart rate of healthy and pathological fetuses differs significantly. Our goal is to determine if this simple metric acts as a reliable standalone indicator, or if relying on it provides a false sense of security.
The data set: 
In this study, we utilized the dataset named "Fetal Health Classification". In this dataset expert obstetricians categorized the 2,126 CTG records. We filter out the ambiguous ‘Suspect’ class to ensure a distinct physiological comparison. The resultant focused dataset comparing 1,655 'Normal' cases against 176 'Pathological' ones. Key features examined include baseline heart rate and uterine contractions, allowing us to isolate specific differences between healthy and distressed fetuses.
Research question:
In this study, we are addressing the following research question:
Is there a statistically significant difference in mean baseline heart rate between 'Normal' and 'Pathological' fetuses? 
Here, baseline heart rate (bpm) is dependent variable and health category (Normal vs. Pathological) is independent variable,  which help us to test for clear physiological distinctions.
Null hypothesis and alternative hypothesis (H0/H1):
We established formal statistical hypotheses to test for a difference in means.
Null Hypothesis (H0): There is no significant difference in the mean baseline heart rate between the Normal and Pathological groups.
	Notation: µNormal=µPathological
Alternative Hypothesis (H1): There is a statistically significant difference in the means between the two groups
	Notation: µNormal ≠ µPathological
We utilized a two-tailed test with a standard significance level (α) of 0.05. We will reject the null hypothesis only if the p-value falls below this threshold.


2	Background research
2.1	Research papers
In this study, we reviewed three relevant research papers to understand the context of analysis. 
Ayres et al. (2000) was pioneers who developed a system named 'SisPorto' which automate the CTG interpretation. They demonstrated that automated feature extraction could match expert consensus and directly produced the dataset. Building on this, Sahin and Subasi (2015) applied advanced Machine Learning to this specific data and achieved high classification accuracy using Neural Networks. Their complex models combine over 20 variables. This black box approach obscures the diagnostic weight of individual features, making it difficult to know which specific metrics are driving the predictions.  Hill and Tushar (2023), on the contrary, offer a clinical reality test. They claim that hypoxia is usually less responsive to baseline heart rate as compared to dynamic data such as variability. The lack of a significant research gap is observed in this literature: although simple features have to be statistically validated when complex AI models ingest all information, they do so in an indiscriminate manner. This is the approach that we take in our study where we test whether the first check in a clinical setting is more likely to have independent diagnostic power when it is solely the baseline rate.
2.2	Why RQ is of interest
In this study we addressed a critical gap between complex machine learning and practical clinical utility. Although such models as those by Sahin and Subasi (2015) are effective in predicting results, they hide the usefulness of other features. Clinicians must understand whether or not baseline heart rate is a valid standalone red flag, or whether a false sense of security is given by use of a baseline heart rate. Our research will identify the way to go next: in case the baseline rate is statistically inconsequential, subsequent studies will have to be centered on dynamic measures, such as heart rate variability, rather than on fixed ones.
 3	Visualisation
3.1	Appropriate graphs for the RQ 
 
Figure 1: Histograms comparing the distribution of baseline heart rate for Normal ($N=1655$) and Pathological ($N=176$) groups
To plot the distribution of the Baseline Heart Rate, we chose to use histograms with density overlays. The given type of plot enables one to immediately compare the center and dispersion of the data across groups. Superimposed bell curve is critical, which presents a visual diagnostic to validate the normality assumption of our use of parametric T-test.

3.2	Additional information relating to understanding the data
The red line shows the theoretical ideal distribution of normal distribution of each group. The visual fit of the data to a standard bell shape may be evaluated by comparing the actual data (blue bars) with this curve. This will enable us to identify skewness or aberrations before attempting to conduct the formal statistical tests.

3.3	Useful information for the data understanding
The standard bell-shaped characteristic of the normal distribution is used in the Normal group. The group of the pathological is multimodal (more than one peak), probably because of the reduced sample size (N=176). But, at least in terms of visual indicators, both distributions are concentrated around the same value (average is around 132 bpm), indicating that the means differ by no apparent amount between the groups. 

